course: dictionary
lesson: 5545
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the words around them tell you which meaning the writer means. This is context. «Rome held this empire together with a professional army, the legions, and with straight paved roads.» Armies and roads go together here, so legions must mean groups of soldiers.
explain: Read the whole sentence before you choose. «Roman roads were built so legions could march fast.» Marching is something soldiers do, so legions are the army units, not the roads. Always ask: which meaning fits here? Then put your meaning into the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
explain: When a word is used in a fancy, formal way, the clues are still there. «Her fans are legion.» The word legion is an adjective here, and it means very many. The sentence is not about soldiers at all, so the clues in the sentence matter more than the first meaning you remember.
fact: Context means the words and sentences around a word.
fact: «Legion» can mean a large army unit in ancient Rome, of about 5,000 soldiers.
fact: «Legion» can also mean a very large number of people or things, as in «a legion of fans».
fact: «Legion» as an adjective is formal and means very many: «Her fans are legion.»
fact: «Legions of» means loads of, as in everyday speech. Check the clues to see which meaning fits.
exercise: «Rome held this empire together with a professional army, the legions, and with straight paved roads.» What do the legions mean here? | Large numbers of tourists | Ancient Roman coins | Straight paved roads | *Units of soldiers in the Roman army
exercise: «Her fans are legion.» What does legion mean in this sentence? | *Very many | Very angry | Very old | Very few
exercise: Which word means a very large group, like legion? | a few | *horde | quiet | single
test: «Roman roads were built so legions could march fast.» What is a legion in this sentence? | A kind of road | *A large unit of the Roman army | A fast horse | A type of Roman coin
test: Nadia says «legions of» means loads of. What does it mean in «legions of fans»? | Very few fans | Angry fans | *Loads of fans | Fans in a row
exam: Which meaning fits best in «Her fans are legion»? | Very old | A type of Roman road | *Very many | A small group of five
